# React Context Architecture

All shared web-side state lives in **nine domain-specific React Contexts** plus
**`StreamShellContext`** (spectator join only) under
`web-viewer-sample-main/src/features/streaming/contexts/`. Six broad-domain
contexts cover most of the app; three narrow feature contexts
(`SpatialSoundContext`, `Video360Context`, `VideoBookSettingsContext`) hold
overlay-specific or session media preferences (the first two: entry, open flag,
per-session onboarding; `VideoBookSettingsContext`: shared videobook / 360 /
spatial caption size and related controls).

## Repository layout vs runtime tree

On disk, `features/streaming/` holds the **viewer shell** (WebRTC, provider
stack, shared widgets/overlays). `features/cu/` and `features/dev/` are
**mode-specific UI** imported by `CuViewLayer` and `DevViewLayer` — they are
**not** above the context providers in the React tree. Runtime order is:
**contexts → shared layers → CU or DEV view layer → leaf components.**

App-wide **CU vs DEV mode** is `AppModeContext` (`src/context/AppModeContext.tsx`),
mounted in `App.tsx`. It is **re-exported** from
`features/streaming/contexts/index.ts` as `useAppMode` / `AppModeProvider` aliases
so consumers can import mode next to domain hooks from one barrel when desired.

## Kit-backed settings and World State Sync

Anything that mirrors **Kit / world configuration** (camera height, weather,
season, time, fog, navmesh mode, movement speed, physics, people visibility,
camera view type, etc.) must live in **one field** on the appropriate domain
context (`Environment`, `Control`, `Navigation`). Dev hamburger panels, CU quick
settings, and CU full settings must **read and write that same field** — no
parallel `useState` for the same knob.

Reconnect and refresh: Kit is authoritative. See `world-state-sync.mdc` —
`worldStateSync` updates the same context setters via `worldStateHandlers.ts`.
Purely **browser** preferences (theme, CU text size, some audio UI) stay out of
world sync as documented there.

## Provider Hierarchy

Providers are nested in `StreamOnlyWindow` in this order (outer → inner).
**Outside** the domain stack, `StreamShellProvider` wraps the spectator join
gate and the stream provider tree (see `contexts/StreamShellContext.tsx`).

```
StreamShellProvider         → spectator join (viewer mode only)
  StreamProvider            → stream connection, loading phases
    EnvironmentProvider     → cameras, weather, sound, fog, sky
```

Full nesting (inside `StreamShellProvider` after join):

```
StreamProvider              → stream connection, loading phases
  EnvironmentProvider       → cameras, weather, sound, fog, sky
    NavigationProvider      → navmesh, seats, exits, restrooms, routes, OSM
      ControlProvider       → control mode, markers, incidents, USD edit,
                              video player + videobook
        SpatialSoundProvider → spatial-sound overlay entry, isOpen,
                              hasOnboarded (per session)
          Video360Provider  → 360° video overlay entry, isOpen,
                              hasOnboarded (per session)
          VideoBookSettingsProvider → session media prefs: caption size index,
                              mute, CC, autoplay, playback speed (see below)
            ChatProvider    → conversation store, AI chat, interaction actions
              AppUIProvider → isViewer, onboarding, scenes, viewport,
                              seat ceremony
```

Each provider **calls its hook internally** (e.g. `EnvironmentProvider` calls
`useEnvironmentHandlers()` and reads `streamReady` from `useStream()`).
No props are needed on providers except `StreamProvider` (needs `userId`,
`onStreamFailed`, `isViewer` from route props) and `AppUIProvider` (needs
`isViewer`).

`SpatialSoundProvider` and `Video360Provider` each own a single `useState`
trio (`entry`, `hasOnboarded`, plus a memoised `isOpen` derived from
`entry !== null`) — no internal handlers hook, no Kit wiring of their own.
Kit events land in `ChatContext`'s interaction-action handlers, which call
`useSpatialSound().open(entry)` / `useVideo360().open(entry)`. The two
providers sit outside `ChatProvider` (so `ChatContext` can read them) and
inside `ControlProvider`.

## Consuming State

Any component inside the provider tree calls the matching hook:

```tsx
const env = useEnvironment();
const nav = useNavigation();
const ctrl = useControl();
const chat = useChat();
const appUI = useAppUI();
const stream = useStream();
const spatialSound = useSpatialSound();
const video360 = useVideo360();
```

**Never pass context-available values as props.** Components read what they need
directly. The only props that should exist are:

- **Instance-specific** — e.g. `JoystickWidget` needs `position="left"|"right"`
  because two instances exist with different handlers.
- **Parent-owned UI routing** — e.g. `closeMenu`, `onClose`, `onOpenSettings`
  where a parent manages local menu/dialog open state.
- **Composition / children** — React `children`, render props.

## Context Files

Each context file in `contexts/` exports three things:

| Export | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `FooProvider` | Provider component (wraps hook, renders `Context.Provider`) |
| `useFoo()` | Consumer hook (throws if used outside provider) |
| `FooContextType` | TypeScript type (auto-derived from the hook return) |

Barrel file: `contexts/index.ts` re-exports everything.

## The 9 Contexts

### `StreamContext` — connection lifecycle
- `streamReady`, `sceneLoading`, `loadingText`, `loadingPhase`
- `handleStreamStarted`, `handleStreamFailed`, `retryConnection`
- `handleSceneSwitch`, `processSceneEvent`, `appStreamKey`
- Hook: `useStreamConnection(userId, onStreamFailed, isViewer)`

### `EnvironmentContext` — world environment
- Camera: `currentCamera`, `handleCameraChange`, `activeFixedCamera`, `placedCameras`
- Weather/sky: `timeOfDay`, `dayOfYear`, `cloudCoverage`, `weatherPreset`, `fogIntensity`
- CU weather: `weather`, `season`, `timeOfDayMinutes` + `apply*` handlers
- Sound: `soundAreasVisible`, `soundCosts`, `soundCostWidgetOpen`
- Camera data: heatmap, tracker, depth capture, pathfinding widget
- Hook: `useEnvironmentHandlers(streamReady)`

### `NavigationContext` — pathfinding & routes
- NavMesh: `navmeshActive`, `navmeshMode`, `navmeshBaking`
- Spots/exits/restrooms/quiet zones: results, routes, move handlers
- Seats: `seatWidgetOpen`, `activeSeatRouteId`, navigate/teleport/measure
- OSM: `osmNavigateOpen`, `osmSelectedPoiId`, route info
- Hook: `useNavigationHandlers(streamReady)`

### `ControlContext` — UI control state
- Mode: `controlMode` (pointClick/wasd/joystick)
- Markers/incidents/cameras: placement flags, click handlers
- People/NPCs: `peopleVisible`, `nearestNpc`, `nearestIcon`
- USD edit: mode, sub-mode, phase, vertex/prim selection, save
- Video player (full-screen 2D overlay): `videoPlayerOpen`, `videoPlayerConfig`, play/close
- Videobook (chapter player): `videobookOpen`, `videobookIconId`
- Hook: `useControlHandlers()`

**Note**: spatial-sound and 360°-video overlay state is **not** here — it
lives in the dedicated `SpatialSoundContext` / `Video360Context` below.

### `ChatContext` — conversation & AI
- `conversation` (per-avatar message store)
- `interactionActions` (dispatch registry for overlays/NPCs/video)
- `chatOpenSource` ('npc' | 'search' | null)
- `avatarAgentTyping`, `welcomeAvatarChatConfig`
- `lastAIResponseText`, `lastAIResponseIsError`
- All chat handlers: `handleChatClose/Send/Back/QuickAction/UserMessage`
- `handleSearchSendToAI`, `handleOpenAssistantChat`

### `SpatialSoundContext` — spatial-sound overlay state
- `entry: SpatialSoundEntry | null` — the current spot's payload (sound URL,
  caption URL, i18n keys, prim path)
- `isOpen: boolean` — derived (`entry !== null`)
- `hasOnboarded: boolean` — true once the user has clicked "Start" in the
  onboarding card during this session; the next open skips the card
- Actions: `open(entry)`, `close()`, `markOnboarded()`
- No internal handlers hook — the provider is just `useState` + memo.
- Consumed by: `SpatialSoundOverlay` (everything), `IconBubbleOverlay`
  (`isOpen` to suppress, `open()` from the bubble click), `ChatContext`
  (registers `spatialSound.open` interaction action → `open()`; hides the
  icon prim while `isOpen`), `useAmbientSoundEngine` (`isOpen` to duck
  ambient gain), `CuViewLayer` (`isOpen` to hide CU chrome).

See `spatial-audio.mdc` for the full pipeline.

### `Video360Context` — 360° video overlay state
- `entry: Video360Entry | null` — the current spot's payload (video URL,
  caption URL, i18n keys, prim path)
- `isOpen: boolean` — derived (`entry !== null`)
- `hasOnboarded: boolean` — true once the user has clicked "Start the
  experience" during this session; the next open skips onboarding
- Actions: `open(entry)`, `close()`, `markOnboarded()`
- No internal handlers hook — same shape as `SpatialSoundContext`.
- Consumed by: `Video360Overlay` (everything), `IconBubbleOverlay`
  (`isOpen` to suppress, `open()` from the bubble click), `ChatContext`
  (registers `video360.open` interaction action → `open()`; hides the
  icon prim while `isOpen`).

See `360-video.mdc` for the full pipeline.

### `VideoBookSettingsContext` — session media preferences (web streaming tree)
- `captionSizeIndex` (0/1/2) — small / default / large caption typography; used by
  `VideoBookSettingsPanel` and by `data-caption-size` on caption strips in
  `VideoBookPlayer`, `Video360Overlay`, and `SpatialSoundOverlay` (shared Figma
  tokens: `video-playback.mdc` § Caption size).
- `autoplayEnabled`, `muted`, `captionsOn`, `playbackSpeedIndex` — same session
  lifetime as the caption index; videobook player and settings panel read/write
  these. 360° / spatial use caption size (and where applicable CC) from here.
- File: `contexts/VideoBookSettingsContext.tsx` — `useVideoBookSettings()`. Provider
  is mounted in `StreamOnlyWindow.tsx` between `Video360Provider` and
  `ChatProvider` so `ChatContext` and all underlays can consume it.

### `AppUIContext` — app-level & seat ceremony
- `isViewer`, `onboardingVisible`, `handleOnboardingDone`
- `invertTouchLook`, `handleInvertTouchLookToggle`
- `availableScenes`, `viewportCaptureStatus`
- Seat ceremony: `seatArrivalCelebrationVisible`, `seatStreamOverlayActive`,
  `seatRouteCuPortalEl`, `closeSeatPanel`, `dismissArrivalCelebration`
- `seatPanelDraft`, `seatForceExpandSignal`, `seatPanelStickyOpen`
- `handleSearchChipNavigate` (opens seat/restroom/quiet widgets from CU search)

## Adding New State

1. **Identify the domain** — which of the 9 contexts does it belong to?
2. **Add to the hook** — e.g. add a `useState` to `useEnvironmentHandlers`.
   The context type updates automatically (`ReturnType<typeof hook>`).
3. **Consume** — any component calls `useEnvironment()` and sees the new field.

If the new state doesn't fit any existing domain, create a new context file
following the same pattern (Provider + hook + type export) and nest it in the
provider stack in `StreamOnlyWindow`.

### When to create a dedicated narrow context vs. extend an existing one

Default to extending an existing context. Create a new narrow context
(`SpatialSoundContext` / `Video360Context` shape) when **all** of:

- State belongs to a single self-contained feature (one overlay, one
  panel, one widget).
- State is consumed by ≥3 unrelated components.
- Feature carries non-trivial UI-preference state (e.g. session-level
  `hasOnboarded`) that belongs in React state rather than module globals.

Narrow-context providers don't need an internal handlers hook — a ~30-line
file with `useState` + `useCallback` + `useMemo` and the exported
`Provider`/`use*`/`*Type`/`*Entry` is the whole shape. Nest the new
provider as a sibling of `ChatProvider` (so `ChatContext` can register
Kit interaction actions that call into it).

## WebRTC canvas (`AppStream`)

`AppStreamCore` (default export from `features/streaming/AppStream.tsx`) is the
low-level WebRTC surface; it accepts the full prop bundle (used by legacy
`connection/Window.tsx`). **`AppStreamConnected`** is the Goteverse entry: it
reads `useStream` / `useEnvironment` / `useNavigation` / `useControl` / `useAppUI`
and passes session fields from `AppProps` plus the Kit event bridge — mount it
only under the provider stack (`StreamOnlyWindowInner`).

## Kit → React Event Bridge

`useCustomEventHandler` in `StreamOnlyWindowInner` wires Kit events to context
setters. It receives setters from the context hooks it wires and maps incoming event types
to state updates. When adding a new Kit event:

1. Add a setter to the appropriate hook (e.g. `useControlHandlers`)
2. Add the mapping in `useCustomEventHandler`'s `EventStateSetters` type
3. Handle the event in the relevant handler file under `hooks/eventHandlers/`
